{
  "schema": "egret.android.v8.native-allocation-profile/1",
  "device": "Samsung Galaxy Z Fold7, arm64; cold start and held frame 210 on the #386 scene",
  "collection": "One start per profile/encoder. The sole data source was android.heapprofd attached to the exact test application. Four independent 14.5 s traces sampled libc.malloc at 65,536 B with 1.5 s scheduled dumps and a 32 MiB client buffer. Standard and trimmed runs used their recorded APKs and unchanged GLES3 renderer.",
  "units": "Allocation triples are [outstanding sampled bytes at the last dump, cumulative sampled allocations in bytes, cumulative sampled frees in bytes]. These are weighted allocator estimates. Sums are over signed callsite deltas. Caller classes partition each trace once. The V8 rows use distinct categories with a matched V8 ancestor; a graphics allocation made under a V8 callback may appear in the inclusive V8 table and the nearest-caller graphics table.",
  "anchors": "The +4 s memory comparison is relative to the first device-boot-clock observation of held frame 210. Completed Perfetto packets were independently matched to their allocation timestamps and summed by callsite. A periodic dump can repeat a preceding sampled state; no new sampled event does not rule out allocations below the sampling interval. Distinct allocation epochs differ from collection timestamps.",
